-- title:
--   Abel–Jacobi-normalised Hecke and Galois action on Pic⁰ of X₁(Mp)
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and an integer $M$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, and let $\zeta \in L$ be a primitive $p$-th root of unity. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq L((q))$ with $K =$ [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), that is, the $L$-subfield of the Laurent series field $L((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L137) of $X_1(Mp)$, the latter being an intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq \mathbb{Q}((q))$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation ring which is a domain with fraction field $L$, such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ (`hAp`) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ in $L$ (`hζA`), with an $A$-algebra structure on $K$ compatible with $A \to L \to K$. Let $j \in K$ be a nonzero element whose image in $L((q))$ is the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) of the modular function $j$ (`hj`). All of this data enters through the two-chart integral model [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, written $X \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ below, which is assumed proper.
--
--   Further data: a section $\varepsilon$ of $X \to \operatorname{Spec} A$, i.e. an element of `SchemeHomOver (𝟙 (Spec (CommRingCat.of A))) (modelTo A K j)`, so a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} A \to X$ splitting the structure morphism; a relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ designation $D$ for $X \to \operatorname{Spec} A$, consisting of a scheme $D.P$, a structure morphism `D.toBase : D.P ⟶ Spec A` and a zero section with `zeroSection ≫ toBase = 𝟙`; and the hypothesis `hrep`, that the type `RepresentsRelSubPic (modelTo A K j) ε (algEquivZeroCut (modelTo A K j) ε) D` is nonempty, i.e. there is a rigidified line bundle (the Poincaré bundle) on the fibre product of $X$ with $D.P$ which is fibrewise algebraically equivalent to zero in the sense of `FibrewiseAlgEquivZero`, is universal for rigidified line bundles with that property, and restricts to the unit along the zero section. It is assumed that `D.toBase` is smooth (`hsm`) and separated (`hsep`).
--
--   Geometric hypotheses over the base changes: $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` is an $A$-algebra through $L$ (compatible algebra and scalar-tower instances). The base change $X_L \to \operatorname{Spec} L$, namely `baseChange A (modelTo A K j) L`, is smooth of relative dimension $1$ (`hsmL`) and geometrically integral (`hgiL`); the base change `pullback.snd D.toBase (specMap A L)` of $D.P$ to $L$ is proper (`hprL`) and geometrically connected (`hgcL`).
--
--   Geometric-fibre identification: $M\eta$ is a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ for the function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) (the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-subfield of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$); thus $M\eta$ carries an integral scheme $M\eta.C$, proper and smooth of relative dimension $1$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, a ring isomorphism `ffEquiv` of that function field with the function field of $M\eta.C$ compatible with the structure morphism, and a bijection `placeOfPoint` from the closed points of $M\eta.C$ onto the places of the function field over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, with the stalk-range and finite-set-in-an-affine-open axioms. The morphism $e\eta$ is an isomorphism from $M\eta.C$ to the fibre product of $X$ with $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, compatible with the structure morphisms (`heη`). The instance `Mη_chart_nonempty` asserts that the preimage under $e\eta$ followed by the first projection of the image of the finite chart [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.ιFin A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L231) is nonempty. The hypothesis `hMηpin` pins this identification by $q$-expansions: for every element $a$ of the finite chart algebra [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.chartAlgFin A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L135), the germ of the pullback of $a$ on that open, transported by `Mη.ffEquiv.symm` into `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` and viewed in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$, is the coefficientwise image under $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the Laurent series of $a$ in $L((q))$. The hypothesis `hgal` asserts Galois equivariance of the point–place dictionary: for every $g \in \operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ fixing the image of $L$ pointwise and for all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, x'$ of $M\eta.C$ (sections of `Mη.toBase`), if $x'$ followed by $e\eta$ and the first projection equals $\operatorname{Spec}(g)$ followed by the same composite for $x$, then `Mη.pointEquivPlace x'` is the image of `Mη.pointEquivPlace x` under the semilinear automorphism [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois (x1FunctionField (M * p)) g`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54) acting on places.
--
--   Hecke data: `hin` is [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsAll (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L58), the conjunction of `HeckeInputsOneAlong (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) (M * p) ℓ` for every prime $\ell$ and, for every $d$ coprime to $M p$, existence of an automorphism of `x1FunctionField (M * p)` over $\mathbb{Q}$ satisfying `IsDiamondAut (M * p) d` together with an automorphism of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ which is a base change of the diamond automorphism; `hcomm` is [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondCommuteBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L54), the pairwise commutation of the generators `heckeDiamondGenBar (M * p) i` for $i$ in `Nat.Primes ⊕ ℕ`. Finally, $\operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ acts on $A$ by semiring automorphisms, compatibly with $A \to L$ (`hΓA`).
--
--   Under these hypotheses there exist a bijection
--   $$\mathrm{gpts} : \mathrm{JOne}(Mp) = \mathrm{Pic}^0\big(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}, \mathrm{x1FunctionFieldBar}\,(M * p)\big) \;\simeq\; \{\,\varphi : \operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to D.P \mid \varphi \circ D.\mathrm{toBase} = \operatorname{Spec}(A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}})\,\},$$
--   a map $\phi$ from [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16) $= \mathbb{Z}[X_i : i \in \mathrm{Nat.Primes} \oplus \mathbb{N}]$ to endomorphisms of $D.P$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, and for each $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ a morphism $\tau s : D.P \to D.P$ over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the action of $s$ on $A$ (an element of `SchemeHomOver (D.toBase ≫ Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (MulSemiringAction.toRingHom (L ≃ₐ[ℚ] L) A s))) D.toBase`), such that the following hold, where the group law on $D$ is `RepresentsRelSubPic.relativeGroupLaw` for a chosen representing datum from `hrep` and the group cut `algEquivZeroGroupCut`.
--
--   First, each $\phi t$ is a homomorphism for that relative group law on all relative points: for every $t$, every scheme $T$ with $s : T \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ and all $x, y$ in `SchemeHomOver s D.toBase`, composing the product $\mathrm{mul}\,s\,x\,y$ with $\phi t$ gives the product of $x$ followed by $\phi t$ and $y$ followed by $\phi t$.
--
--   Second, with the module structure [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleOneBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L129) on $\mathrm{JOne}(Mp)$ installed, for every $t$ and every $x$ the underlying morphism of $\mathrm{gpts}(t \cdot x)$ equals that of $\mathrm{gpts}(x)$ followed by $\phi t$.
--
--   Third, $(\tau 1)$ is the identity of $D.P$, and $(\tau(s s'))$ is $\tau s$ followed by $\tau s'$ for all $s, s' \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$.
--
--   Fourth, $\tau s$ and $\phi t$ commute as morphisms $D.P \to D.P$, for all $t$ and $s$.
--
--   Fifth, $\mathrm{gpts}$ is additive: $\mathrm{gpts}(x + y)$ is the group-law product of $\mathrm{gpts}(x)$ and $\mathrm{gpts}(y)$.
--
--   Sixth, $\mathrm{gpts}$ is Galois-semilinear: for $\sigma' \in \operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ and $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ with $\sigma'$ restricting on the image of $L$ to $s$, and for every $x \in \mathrm{JOne}(Mp)$, the morphism underlying $\mathrm{gpts}(\sigma' \cdot x)$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}(\sigma')$ followed by $\mathrm{gpts}(x)$ followed by $\tau(s^{-1})$.
--
--   Seventh, the Abel–Jacobi normalisation: there exist a representing datum $h_{DL}$ of `RepresentsRelSubPic` for the base change $X_L \to \operatorname{Spec} L$ with section `sectionBaseChange L ε`, the condition `algEquivZeroCut` of that base change and the designation `D.baseChange L`; a morphism $\mathrm{aj}_L$ from $X_L$ to $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\ L).P$ over $\operatorname{Spec} L$; a morphism $k_L$ from the fibre product of $X$ with $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ to the fibre product of $X$ with $\operatorname{Spec} L$; a morphism $\overline{\mathrm{aj}} : M\eta.C \to D.P$; and a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $\overline{\varepsilon}$ of $M\eta.C$, subject to the following clauses. The Poincaré bundle of $h_{DL}$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of `BaseChange.ofR` applied to the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of the chosen datum along the first projection of $D.P \times_{\operatorname{Spec} A} \operatorname{Spec} L$. The section `sectionBaseChange L ε` followed by $\mathrm{aj}_L$ is the zero section of `D.baseChange L`. For every field $K'$, every $t : \operatorname{Spec} K' \to \operatorname{Spec} L$ and every $x$ in `SchemeHomOver t (baseChange A (modelTo A K j) L)`, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of $h_{DL}$ along $x$ followed by $\mathrm{aj}_L$ is isomorphic to the tensor product of the `lineBundle` of the relative effective Cartier divisor `RelEffCartierDiv.ofPoint` of the point $x$ with the `idealModule` of `RelEffCartierDiv.ofPoint` of the point $t$ followed by `sectionBaseChange L ε`; thus $\mathrm{aj}_L$ classifies the class of $x$ minus the cusp section. The comparison morphism $k_L$ satisfies $k_L$ followed by the first projection equals the first projection, and $k_L$ followed by the second projection equals the second projection followed by $\operatorname{Spec}(L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}})$. Moreover $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$ equals $e\eta$ followed by $k_L$, by $\mathrm{aj}_L$ and by the first projection of $D.P \times_{\operatorname{Spec} A}\operatorname{Spec} L$; $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$ followed by `D.toBase` equals `Mη.toBase` followed by $\operatorname{Spec}(A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}})$; $\overline{\varepsilon}$ followed by $e\eta$ and the first projection equals $\operatorname{Spec}(A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ followed by $\varepsilon$; and $\overline{\varepsilon}$ followed by $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}(A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ followed by the zero section of $D$. Finally, for all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x$ and $s$ of $M\eta.C$ such that $s$ followed by $e\eta$ and the first projection equals $\operatorname{Spec}(A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ followed by $\varepsilon$, there is a divisor $D_v$ in the degree-zero subgroup of the divisors of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ whose underlying finitely supported function is the difference of the indicator of the place `Mη.pointEquivPlace x` and the indicator of the place `Mη.pointEquivPlace s`, and such that the morphism underlying $\mathrm{gpts}$ of the class of $D_v$ equals $x$ followed by $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$.
--
--   This is the generic-fibre dictionary for the Jacobian of $X_1(Mp)$ in the form used downstream: it identifies $J_1(Mp)(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$, presented as degree-zero divisor classes of the function field, with the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of a relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$-model over the discrete valuation ring $A$ above $p$, equips that model with Hecke and diamond endomorphisms and with semilinear Galois transport, and fixes the identification by the Abel–Jacobi normalisation $x \mapsto [x - \varepsilon]$ relative to the cusp section. It is used in the construction of the $q$-expansion semistable specialisation data for $X_1(Mp)$ with its Hecke and diamond structure.
